    "lawCode": "Amd §71-0907, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S1595A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the environmental conservation law,\nin relation to the powers and duties of enforcement officers\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nRelates to the powers and duties of enforcement officers.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSubdivision 4 of Section 71-0907 of the Environmental Conservation\nLaw, subparagraph 5 of paragraph f as added by Chapter 366 of the Laws\nof 1981, is amended to relating to the powers and duties of State New\nYork State Department of Environmental Conservation (DEC) enforcement\nofficers with respect to executing all warrants and search warrants\nissued for violations of the Fish and Wildlife Law.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThis legislation would require \"probable cause\" for a warrantless\nsearch and would require a conservation officer to file a sworn\naffidavit with the court within 24 hours of the search that describes\nthe basis for probable cause. Any persons charged pursuant to such a\nsearch would have the ability to challenge the sufficiency of probable\ncause in court.\n\nThe genesis of this legislation was the practice of warrantless\nsearches and seizures by the DEC, where fish were seized from\ncommercial fishermen and sold by the DEC, only to have the court case\ninvolving the fish subsequently dismissed without the DEC ever making\nrestitution. This prompted an investigation by the State Inspector\nGeneral (IG) who several years later, issued a report and the DEC made\nrestitution to several fishermen. However, the IG report never\naddressed the underlying constitutional issues related to these\nwarrantless searches, leaving the issue to the State Legislature.\n\nThere is no constitutional exemption from the 4th Amendment of the\nUnited States Constitution for the State DEC. As such, the DEC must\nhave probable cause for warrantless searches and they should be\nrequired to document the basis for probable cause to search. The\ncurrent state law does not meet this standard, so this legislation is\nnecessary to remedy this infirmity. This legislation still permits the\nDEC to enforce the law efficiently, but would also protect commercial\nfishermen from undue invasions of personal privacy where the\ngovernment had no real basis to conduct a search.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2013-14: S.3060/A.3889\n2012:S.6876/A.9751\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nTo be determined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
